16","ROBOTIC SAMPLE RETURN · FLOWN","First robotic lunar sample return; first non-American lunar samples","Luna 16 was the Soviet Union's first successful robotic sample-return mission and the first non-crewed lunar return of any kind. After Apollo 11 brought home 22 kg of crewed samples in 1969, Luna 16 returned 101 grams of regolith from Mare Fecunditatis a year later — a fraction of Apollo's payload, but a profound proof-of-concept for the alternative architecture. The mission used a Proton-K booster and a direct return profile: descent to the surface, drill core, ascent stage launch, ballistic return capsule to Kazakhstan. The technique was reused for Luna 20 and Luna 24.",[516,520,523,527,530],{"met":64,"label":517,"note":518,"type":519},"LAUNCH","Proton-K from Baikonur 1970-09-12.","nominal",{"met":69,"label":521,"note":522,"type":519},"TLI","Trans-lunar injection on the Blok-D upper stage.",{"met":74,"label":524,"note":525,"type":526},"TCM","Mid-course correction during 4.5-day cruise.","info",{"met":12,"label":528,"note":529,"type":519},"LANDING","Soft landing in Mare Fecunditatis 1970-09-20. Surface ops + drill core (101 g).",{"met":83,"label":531,"note":532,"type":519},"SAMPLE RETURN","Capsule recovered in Kazakhstan 1970-09-24. First non-crewed lunar sample return.","/orrery/images/missions/luna16/05.webp"],"uses":{"params":["id"]}}]}
